00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.860
to make people stand accountable,

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:03.020
to highlight the issues that
you should be thinking about

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.690
when you're voting or going
about your day-to-day life.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.970
It's meant to give you
important information

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.239
about things like, I
don't know, pandemics.

00:01:10.239 --> 00:01:12.990
(upbeat music)

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.110
- Clearly Nickelodeon is
pushing a global warming agenda.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.630
- In short, it's meant to
keep the people informed

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.640
and aware of what is
important in the world.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.270
But as a news consumer myself

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.470
and as someone who wants to be informed

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.020
and to understand how the world works,

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.860
I find that the news less and less

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.630
and less actually helps me do that.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.463
- It is not the newspaper-

00:01:34.463 --> 00:01:39.463
- But now you're pedaling in dishonesties

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:40.667
- So I've been thinking about this

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.150
and I wanna share it with you.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:46.170
I'm gonna give you three
reasons why I hate the news.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.950
Number one, the news often tells you

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.600
what isn't actually
happening in the world.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.710
There's this onion article
that just sums it up

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:59.710
so well .00003% of things
that happen actually matter.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:04.040
Oftentimes we look at the
news and we see crazy things

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.140
that are happening around the world.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.290
If there is a mass homicide,

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:10.600
that would be national
news tomorrow morning,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.310
if there's a terrorist
attack around the world,

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.610
that will be national
news tomorrow morning.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:15.844
If a prominent politician

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:18.070
says something really embarrassing,

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.550
that will be national
news tomorrow morning.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:21.827
- What's the naughtiest
thing you ever did?

00:02:21.827 --> 00:02:23.890
- I have to confess when me and my friends

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.790
sort of used to run through
the fields of wheat,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.050
the farmers weren't
too pleased about that.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.510
- What all these things
have in common is that

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:30.670
they don't happen very often,

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:31.760
they're super rare.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.560
They don't teach us anything about

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.610
how the world actually works,

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.670
they teach us about how
the world doesn't work

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.690
and how sometimes it does,
the outliers, the aberrations.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.210
My big problem with this is that someone

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.510
who reads the news every day
and uses it as their gateway

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.560
into the world will often walk away

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.940
with a completely delusional version

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.210
of how the world works.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.460
They will assume that terrible
things happen more often

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:55.620
than they actually do.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.040
Our view of the world is at stake here.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.822
When the information available to us

00:03:00.822 --> 00:03:03.460
is the stuff that doesn't
happen very often,

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.890
the stuff that's actually
an aberration from the norm.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.560
We get a distorted view
of how the world works.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.980
Number two, the news
has become way too fun.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.760
- [Man] Meeting with
the pirate, no problem.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.740
- [Announcer] Tonight,
president Donald Trump

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.219
fighting for a second term as
a deadly pandemic gets worse.

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:22.500
- This is not a legal proceeding.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.496
- This is the congress of the united-

00:03:23.496 --> 00:03:25.520
- This is an impeachment.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.040
- My old colleague, Carlos
Maza has thought a lot

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.620
about how fun the news has become.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.500
I called him up and we chatted about it.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:32.333
How are you?

00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:33.540
- I know good to see your face.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.480
See for longest, as long as
you've had corporate news

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.490
in America is that we have a product

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:41.160
that is trying to meet two
very different objectives

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.770
or a suit, a very different incentive.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.240
- What Carlos explained to
me that was really helpful

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.470
was that the news has
two opposing incentives.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.210
The first incentive is what I
talked about at the beginning

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.540
of this video, which is
to inform the people,

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.670
to teach you what you should care about,

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.490
to give you a framework for
understanding the world,

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:00.360
for highlighting important information

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.440
the public should know,

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.960
but then there's this other incentive.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.830
- The corporate media is
trying to sell you a product

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.220
and they sell your
product to make you think

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.621
you need as much of it as
humanly possible all the time.

00:04:09.621 --> 00:04:11.230
(upbeat music)

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.380
So newscasts should be
maybe an hour every day

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.060
and then you're done
like, go do your thing.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.350
You have this other incentive though,

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:18.640
while you have a corporate
media environment

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.534
that is like always breaking.

00:04:20.534 --> 00:04:23.760
(logo squishing)

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.630
There's always a new thing
that you have to worry about.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.730
So you have this basically
impossible impasse

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.210
of a product that you should
not really need that much,

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.861
that shows a specific function
being run by companies

00:04:32.861 --> 00:04:35.120
that want you to need it all the time.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.231
- I am not distracting.

00:04:36.231 --> 00:04:38.740
- You've been doing it for
over a year and a half.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:39.820
- I haven't been doing that.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:40.780
- Well you have.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.180
- What this does, is it
incentivize programming

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.360
that is optimized to appeal to our senses,

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.070
whether it's fear based sensationalism

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:52.980
or whether it's flashy graphics,

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.910
article titles that are
a little bit click baity.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:59.460
The economic incentive
that is built into news

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.810
makes it really difficult
for news to honor

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.160
that first incentive.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.230
- I call them a moron,

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:08.110
and then I modified
that word with a vulgar

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:11.390
Anglo-Saxons term that is
also intelligible in dutch.

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.090
- And instead, to prioritize entertainment

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:18.227
and false feelings of this is
important or this is scary.

00:05:18.227 --> 00:05:20.140
(upbeat music)

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.470
- [Announcer] This is CNN breaking news.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:23.840
- It seems like a really dangerous thing

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.700
to be so subject to economic forces.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.030
Okay, my number three is
that the news tricks you

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.920
into feeling informed.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.820
For a long time I read
the news every single day,

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.780
I would go on and read
multiple different outlets

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.770
and I would feel that day like check.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.850
Okay, I believe that I'm
an informed smart person

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.960
and I've just sort of
confirmed that belief

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.180
because there's this sort
of like glorification

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.856
of the person who like
really up on the daily news.

00:05:48.856 --> 00:05:51.840
What I've learned is that
that's actually not the case.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.310
- Why don't people just check out

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.100
and that's a legitimate response,

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:58.100
like if the town prior in your
village constantly screaming,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.450
10,000 horrific things at
you from other villages

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.140
that you don't live in,
after all you'd stop

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.500
really meaningfully listening to it

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.100
because your brain can not
sort out what catastrophe

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.010
is real and meaningful and
what you shouldn't respond to.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.700
So you have this like kind
of ambient anxiety always

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.110
that makes it so that your
brain becomes numb to the drug.

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.000
You stop really caring about
what you should engage in.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.692
- Spending a lot of time
chasing the new cycle

00:06:19.692 --> 00:06:21.460
is a lot of mental energy.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:23.670
It's a lot of mental energy
that could be applied

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:27.280
to understanding how the
world actually works,

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.460
consuming things that actually shed light

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.810
on important issues like,
policy or I don't know,

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:36.110
historical trends that maybe
are not as flashy and reactive,

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.770
but are way more indicative
on how the world works,

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:43.730
how things get done, how
change gets made in a society.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:46.210
Instead because of this
entertainment incentive,

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.950
the news doesn't actually end
up being super informative.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.440
You're not actually learning
a lot about anything

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.190
other than what happened that
day, who said what to whom

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:59.480
and why, and then how a
billion people reacted

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.330
to why that person said whom.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:04.180
And it's like, it becomes a
giant gossip first almost.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:05.715
- Binge watch like the
Rockford files, Netflix.

00:07:05.715 --> 00:07:08.572
- Or modern love
- That's terrible.

00:07:08.572 --> 00:07:11.070
Modern love makes me sick.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.570
- How about person of interest?

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:13.920
- Ooh, I like that,

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:14.770
that's a good show.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:15.603
- Can I say something now?

00:07:15.603 --> 00:07:16.436
- Yes.
- No!

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:19.730
- It's built to appeal
more to our gossip impulse

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.795
than to our desire to
learn about the world.

00:07:21.795 --> 00:07:25.510
The epidemy of this is the sort
of like pseudo debate format

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:28.860
that a lot of TV news takes apart.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.370
- The five person talking head panel,

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:34.460
you have moderator who pretends
to be relatively neutral

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.330
and then four commentators
two from the left,

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:37.850
two from the right who
are arguing about whatever

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:38.683
the topic of the day is.

00:07:38.683 --> 00:07:42.210
That format is a straight rip from ESPN.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:46.868
Like those formats are a
carbon copy of the arguing

00:07:46.868 --> 00:07:50.700
and almost like a reality
TV model that has been used

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.348
in sports media for a while.

00:07:52.348 --> 00:07:53.950
- This is not journalism.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:56.130
This looks like journalism
because it looks like there's

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:57.780
a robust debate going on.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.516
- Stop it, you're abusing
power right now on air.

00:08:00.516 --> 00:08:02.000
Stop filibustering her.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.996
- But journalism is about evidence.

00:08:03.996 --> 00:08:06.880
It's about going out
and gathering evidence

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.904
and scrutinizing it and
then putting it together

00:08:08.904 --> 00:08:10.930
and presenting it to a reader.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.360
In this sensationalized news segment,

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.660
no evidence is being
examined or scrutinized

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:17.930
or cross-examined.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.380
- It serves no educational function,

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:20.880
regardless of what you
think about politics,

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.080
watching two people
argue is not informative

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:24.598
because it's too fast paced,

00:08:24.598 --> 00:08:26.330
it triggers your tribal defenses,

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:27.590
you're not gonna be open-minded

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.170
and there's no time to examine evidence,

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:30.650
no matter how long that fight goes on for,

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.760
you are not gonna learn
anything meaningful from it.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:34.527
All will happen is that
you will leave thinking,

00:08:34.527 --> 00:08:35.880
"God, I really hate the other side."

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.530
And God by sight is so right.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:39.171
It's the opposite of what
journalism should be doing.

00:08:39.171 --> 00:08:42.250
- You don't leave a debate
like this anymore informed,

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.650
all you do is leave a debate like this

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:47.230
with like an understanding
that your side is right,

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.450
the other side is wrong
and like how awesome

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:51.370
that like the people who
represented your side

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:53.800
were like so hard on the opposing side,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.210
which is a super awesome
impulse if you're like

00:08:56.210 --> 00:09:00.220
in a football stadium watching
your favorite team play.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.005
But when we're talking
about like real issues

00:09:02.005 --> 00:09:06.560
and real policy, this sports
framework of commentary

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.080
and fighting is not very useful

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:10.790
because it tricks people into thinking

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:11.987
that they're informed, that they're like,

00:09:11.987 --> 00:09:13.970
"oh, I get the issues now
'cause I watched that debate."

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.690
But instead all they were was entertained.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:20.660
- I'm tired of having this
discussion over and over again.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:23.840
- So those are my three reasons
for why I hate the news.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:25.130
So let me just say one thing,

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.760
which is there is still amazing journalism

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.080
being done right now,
really important journalism

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.410
that is communicating important evidence,

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:37.010
about important ideas
that truly can inform you

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.320
about how the world works.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.720
The incentives of this industry
make it harder and harder

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.500
for that journalism to happen.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.050
But the journalists who are
out there collecting evidence,

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:48.320
reporting on really difficult things,

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.360
talking to lots of experts,
trying to piece together,

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.590
all of this information so that
they can inform the public.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.250
Those are the people
who are doing this work

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:00.500
in spite of the entertainment,
corporate 24 hour news cycle

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.740
that has sort of taken
over what news should be.

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:06.410
And what I really believe
and I learned this

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:10.170
working at Vox, a place that
really focused on context

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:12.530
and explainer journalism,
there's a demand for it,

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:15.289
there will always be a
demand of voyeuristic demand

00:10:15.289 --> 00:10:18.180
for seeing terrible things happen.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.750
There will always be
demand for entertainment

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:23.510
and shouting matches, but
I believe that most people

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:25.630
want to understand,
they want to understand

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.880
why this certain policy will
work and the other one won't.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:31.230
They want to understand the real debates,

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.120
the philosophical and political debates

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.910
that are happening in our system.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.640
They wanna understand how our system works

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:38.473
or doesn't work.

00:10:38.473 --> 00:10:41.420
And if only our news
industry could focus more

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:44.000
on that desire, that desire to understand,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.670
instead of this facade of information

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:49.210
that is really just entertainment.

